1
00:00:00,600 --> 00:00:01,709
Hi everyone,

2
00:00:01,729 --> 00:00:08,486
and welcome back to Sewing Machine Design and Manufacturing at WenQuest.

3
00:00:08,886 --> 00:00:13,826
This is Lecture 9, the overlock machine.

4
00:00:14,226 --> 00:00:19,046
Turn a knitted T-shirt inside out and look at the side seam.

5
00:00:19,066 --> 00:00:23,160
The edge is wrapped in a dense row of thread loops,

6
00:00:23,180 --> 00:00:29,130
sewn by an overlock machine. It has no rotary hook, its needle is slanted,

7
00:00:29,150 --> 00:00:34,212
two loopers swing under the plate, and there are two feed dogs.

8
00:00:34,232 --> 00:00:38,327
Today we'll see why each is designed the way it is.

9
00:00:39,847 --> 00:00:40,907
The shop-floor problem.

10
00:00:41,307 --> 00:00:48,080
A knitwear factory joins jersey T-shirt side seams on a high-speed

11
00:00:48,100 --> 00:00:50,551
three-thread overlocker,

12
00:00:50,571 --> 00:00:54,668
with the differential ratio left at 1.0.

13
00:00:55,068 --> 00:01:01,073
Inspection finds the seam edge bulging into waves about 30 millimetres long.

14
00:01:01,473 --> 00:01:09,221
Test sewing shows the edge stretches about 18 percent at a ratio of 1,

15
00:01:09,241 --> 00:01:16,213
and only about 0.85 of the extra dog travel reaches the fabric.

16
00:01:16,613 --> 00:01:24,291
Next week they'll sew rib necklines: rib stretches about 30 percent,

17
00:01:24,311 --> 00:01:27,687
with a fraction of about 0.80.

18
00:01:28,087 --> 00:01:32,626
How high are the waves, and what ratio should each fabric use?

19
00:01:34,146 --> 00:01:37,505
A machine made for knits.

20
00:01:39,025 --> 00:01:43,645
Why not sew a T-shirt side seam with a lockstitch?

21
00:01:44,045 --> 00:01:44,945
Three reasons.

22
00:01:45,345 --> 00:01:50,108
Elasticity: jersey stretches more than 30 percent,

23
00:01:50,128 --> 00:01:54,986
while a lockstitch barely stretches and would snap.

24
00:01:55,386 --> 00:02:01,153
Edge finishing: a knit edge curls and runs once it's cut,

25
00:02:01,173 --> 00:02:07,041
and the overlocker trims, seams and overcasts in one pass.

26
00:02:07,441 --> 00:02:16,163
Speed and continuity: thread comes straight from big cones, there's no bobbin,

27
00:02:16,183 --> 00:02:19,861
and the shaft reaches 7000 r/min.

28
00:02:20,261 --> 00:02:24,881
These needs decide the whole structure of the machine.

29
00:02:26,401 --> 00:02:33,381
Make the housing transparent and you see that every mechanism hangs on one

30
00:02:33,401 --> 00:02:34,421
main shaft.

31
00:02:34,821 --> 00:02:40,643
It runs left to right through the base, with the handwheel at the right.

32
00:02:41,043 --> 00:02:45,927
One eccentric pushes the lower-looper shaft through a ball-joint link,

33
00:02:45,947 --> 00:02:51,321
another the upper-looper rocker; feed and knife are driven by eccentrics too,

34
00:02:51,341 --> 00:02:54,263
and the needle bar by a crank in the head.

35
00:02:54,663 --> 00:03:01,541
There's no upper shaft, lower shaft and timing belt as on a lockstitch machine,

36
00:03:01,561 --> 00:03:04,858
so it's compact and can run very fast.

37
00:03:06,378 --> 00:03:12,477
Here the edge is drawn unfolded: the face on top, the edge in the middle,

38
00:03:12,497 --> 00:03:13,734
the back below.

39
00:03:14,134 --> 00:03:17,754
The needle thread goes through the fabric every stitch.

40
00:03:18,154 --> 00:03:24,652
The lower-looper thread passes through the needle loop on the back and runs

41
00:03:24,672 --> 00:03:25,694
to the edge.

42
00:03:26,094 --> 00:03:29,967
There it interlocks with the upper-looper thread,

43
00:03:29,987 --> 00:03:35,528
which runs back across the face and is held by the next needle stitch.

44
00:03:35,928 --> 00:03:39,820
Three threads, three jobs, no tight knot;

45
00:03:39,840 --> 00:03:46,308
under tension the threads slide at the loops, so the seam stretches.

46
00:03:46,708 --> 00:03:59,568
514 adds a second needle and is stronger; 516 is a 401 chainstitch plus a 504.

47
00:04:01,088 --> 00:04:03,490
Three loop entries in one stitch.

48
00:04:05,010 --> 00:04:09,230
A lockstitch needs one critical hand-over per stitch;

49
00:04:09,250 --> 00:04:11,710
an overlock stitch needs three.

50
00:04:12,110 --> 00:04:17,048
First, at 213.6 degrees, with the needle 2.6 millimetres up,

51
00:04:17,068 --> 00:04:22,667
the lower-looper point slips behind the needle into the needle loop,

52
00:04:22,687 --> 00:04:25,311
with 0.05 millimetres clearance.

53
00:04:25,711 --> 00:04:27,749
Second, at 270.8,

54
00:04:27,769 --> 00:04:36,466
the upper looper passes between the lower looper's blade and its thread,

55
00:04:36,486 --> 00:04:38,282
0.10 clearance.

56
00:04:38,682 --> 00:04:40,684
Third, at 57.5,

57
00:04:40,704 --> 00:04:49,581
the needle passes between the upper looper's blade and its thread.

58
00:04:49,981 --> 00:04:53,401
Each time, a point goes through a triangle:

59
00:04:53,421 --> 00:04:57,241
the timing must be right and the clearance tiny.

60
00:04:58,761 --> 00:05:02,353
This is the stitch-zone close-up of Model 9-1,

61
00:05:02,373 --> 00:05:07,301
with the shaft turning slowly and the needle-point path traced.

62
00:05:07,701 --> 00:05:12,121
Watch the lower looper swing from right to left,

63
00:05:12,141 --> 00:05:18,411
brushing past behind the needle; the upper looper rises straight up,

64
00:05:18,431 --> 00:05:23,869
then reaches over the edge to the upper left of the needle.

65
00:05:24,269 --> 00:05:28,030
The loop and the triangles are open only briefly:

66
00:05:28,050 --> 00:05:32,969
too early and they're not open yet, too late and they've closed.

67
00:05:33,369 --> 00:05:38,716
Too much clearance and the point misses the thread, a skipped stitch;

68
00:05:38,736 --> 00:05:42,761
too little and it scrapes or even breaks the needle.

69
00:05:44,281 --> 00:05:48,655
The slanted needle and the looper's spatial four-bar.

70
00:05:50,175 --> 00:05:51,457
Why slant the needle?

71
00:05:51,857 --> 00:05:54,428
The main reason is the lower looper.

72
00:05:54,828 --> 00:06:01,168
It must brush past behind the needle and stay close to it while the needle rises.

73
00:06:01,568 --> 00:06:05,557
If it swings about an axis parallel to the needle,

74
00:06:05,577 --> 00:06:10,769
its point moves on an arc in a plane perpendicular to the needle,

75
00:06:10,789 --> 00:06:15,099
so the distance stays the same wherever the needle is,

76
00:06:15,119 --> 00:06:18,948
and a 0.05-millimetre clearance is easy to hold.

77
00:06:19,348 --> 00:06:21,990
Slant the needle and the shaft slants with it.

78
00:06:22,390 --> 00:06:25,867
With 1.5 millimetres of fabric,

79
00:06:25,887 --> 00:06:31,959
the needle enters at 80.8 degrees and leaves at 279.2,

80
00:06:31,979 --> 00:06:35,570
leaving 162 degrees for feeding.

81
00:06:37,090 --> 00:06:43,389
The lower-looper shaft is parallel to the needle, at 20 degrees,

82
00:06:43,409 --> 00:06:47,339
while the main shaft runs left to right.

83
00:06:47,739 --> 00:06:54,293
The two axes neither intersect nor are parallel, so a planar four-bar won't do;

84
00:06:54,313 --> 00:06:56,956
it has to be a spatial four-bar.

85
00:06:57,356 --> 00:07:04,038
The crank turns with the main shaft, radius 4.36 millimetres;

86
00:07:04,058 --> 00:07:12,169
the link with a ball joint at each end is 22.48 long; the rocker, 22 long,

87
00:07:12,189 --> 00:07:15,465
swings about the looper shaft.

88
00:07:15,865 --> 00:07:20,419
Two revolute joints and two spherical joints: RSSR.

89
00:07:20,819 --> 00:07:27,559
On the right is the 3D animation from Virtual Lab 9-2.

90
00:07:29,079 --> 00:07:32,537
How do we get the speed?

91
00:07:32,937 --> 00:07:39,750
The two ends of a rigid link have equal velocity components along the link.

92
00:07:40,150 --> 00:07:46,946
That gives theta-dot as d dot A-dot over d dot t.

93
00:07:47,346 --> 00:07:49,810
When the denominator goes to zero,

94
00:07:49,830 --> 00:07:53,096
it's a dead point and speed and load blow up;

95
00:07:53,116 --> 00:07:57,113
the transmission angle measures how far we are from it.

96
00:07:57,513 --> 00:08:04,987
At 7000 r/min the rocker peaks at about 182 radians per second,

97
00:08:05,007 --> 00:08:10,460
the looper tip at about 6.9 metres per second,

98
00:08:10,480 --> 00:08:16,526
and the minimum transmission angle is 49.5 degrees,

99
00:08:16,546 --> 00:08:20,333
above the usual 40-degree limit.

100
00:08:20,733 --> 00:08:24,966
Beyond a crank radius of about 10.6 millimetres,

101
00:08:24,986 --> 00:08:28,599
the mechanism can no longer be assembled.

102
00:08:30,119 --> 00:08:34,019
Differential feed: keeping the edge flat.

103
00:08:35,539 --> 00:08:41,343
An overlocker has one feed dog behind the needle and one in front.

104
00:08:41,743 --> 00:08:48,030
The main dog behind the needle feeds one stitch length s each stitch;

105
00:08:48,050 --> 00:08:51,686
the differential dog in front feeds s_d.

106
00:08:52,086 --> 00:08:56,786
Their ratio is the differential ratio D.

107
00:08:57,186 --> 00:08:59,103
With D greater than 1,

108
00:08:59,123 --> 00:09:04,739
the front dog feeds more and crowds the fabric between the dogs,

109
00:09:04,759 --> 00:09:07,646
cancelling the stretch of a knit.

110
00:09:08,046 --> 00:09:15,308
With D less than 1 it feeds less and gently pulls a slippery woven apart.

111
00:09:15,708 --> 00:09:23,408
Here D sweeps between 0.8 and 1.6; watch how the two strokes change.

112
00:09:24,928 --> 00:09:26,969
When knit fabric is sewn,

113
00:09:26,989 --> 00:09:32,823
the presser foot drags the top ply back and the main dog pulls forward,

114
00:09:32,843 --> 00:09:38,924
so the edge is stretched, and the stitches fix it in that stretched state.

115
00:09:39,324 --> 00:09:44,656
Let go and the fabric shrinks back, but the thread doesn't,

116
00:09:44,676 --> 00:09:51,641
so the extra length bulges into waves, the so-called lettuce or ruffled edge.

117
00:09:52,041 --> 00:09:58,321
A simple model: at D equal to 1 the edge stretches by epsilon-zero,

118
00:09:58,341 --> 00:10:04,621
and only a fraction eta of the extra dog travel reaches the fabric.

119
00:10:05,021 --> 00:10:13,586
Setting the edge strain to zero gives the flat-edge ratio D-star equals 1

120
00:10:13,606 --> 00:10:16,761
plus epsilon-zero over eta.

121
00:10:17,161 --> 00:10:25,141
Treat the wave as a sine, and its height is about lambda root epsilon over pi.

122
00:10:26,661 --> 00:10:31,921
This is the jersey edge as sewn in Virtual Lab 9-1.

123
00:10:32,321 --> 00:10:37,021
On the left, ratio 1.0: the edge is wavy.

124
00:10:37,421 --> 00:10:43,761
On the right, set to 1.2: the waves have basically gone.

125
00:10:44,161 --> 00:10:49,844
The differential lever on the outside of the machine moves a slider in the

126
00:10:49,864 --> 00:10:52,618
slotted arm of the differential dog;

127
00:10:52,638 --> 00:10:55,701
slide it outwards and the ratio goes up.

128
00:10:56,101 --> 00:11:03,740
A typical high-speed overlocker adjusts from 1 to 0.7 up to 1 to 2.

129
00:11:05,260 --> 00:11:12,751
The lower-looper RSSR turns shaft angle into swing angle with one constraint:

130
00:11:12,771 --> 00:11:15,482
the link length is constant.

131
00:11:15,882 --> 00:11:22,322
Each leg of a Delta parallel robot is also revolute, spherical, spherical,

132
00:11:22,342 --> 00:11:26,862
and its controller solves the same kind of equation.

133
00:11:27,262 --> 00:11:32,913
The position equation has a plus and a minus solution, the two assembly modes,

134
00:11:32,933 --> 00:11:38,003
just like elbow-up and elbow-down in a robot arm's inverse kinematics;

135
00:11:38,023 --> 00:11:40,984
the program must pick one and stay on it.

136
00:11:41,384 --> 00:11:45,659
When d dot t goes to zero you're at a singularity,

137
00:11:45,679 --> 00:11:51,671
and robot designers likewise keep the working range well away from it.

138
00:11:53,191 --> 00:11:56,451
Differential feed shows up at home too.

139
00:11:56,851 --> 00:12:04,431
Hem a knit T-shirt on a home machine and the edge often comes out wavy.

140
00:12:04,831 --> 00:12:11,369
Experienced sewers gently push the fabric towards the needle with their

141
00:12:11,389 --> 00:12:15,896
fingers in front of the presser foot as they sew.

142
00:12:16,296 --> 00:12:19,724
The fingers are the differential feed dog:

143
00:12:19,744 --> 00:12:25,800
feeding in a little extra fabric cancels the stretch caused by the drag of

144
00:12:25,820 --> 00:12:27,196
the presser foot.

145
00:12:28,716 --> 00:12:30,616
Back to the start.

146
00:12:31,016 --> 00:12:37,150
Jersey side seams at a ratio of 1.0 have 18 percent edge strain,

147
00:12:37,170 --> 00:12:43,015
giving waves about 4.1 millimetres high, visible at a glance.

148
00:12:43,415 --> 00:12:49,042
The flat-edge ratio is 1 plus epsilon-zero over eta:

149
00:12:49,062 --> 00:12:53,278
about 1.21 for jersey and 1.38 for rib;

150
00:12:53,298 --> 00:12:59,034
rib at 1 by mistake would wave about 4.5 millimetres.

151
00:12:59,434 --> 00:13:05,974
Both are within the machine's 1 to 0.7 up to 1 to 2 range.

152
00:13:06,374 --> 00:13:09,754
Remember epsilon-zero and eta are illustrative;

153
00:13:09,774 --> 00:13:12,936
in practice you measure them by test sewing.

154
00:13:14,456 --> 00:13:17,581
Now Virtual lab 9-1, differential feed.

155
00:13:17,601 --> 00:13:23,144
On the WenQuest platform you can do it yourself. The effective ratio,

156
00:13:23,164 --> 00:13:29,353
the edge strain, the wave height, the verdict and the tasks are blurred here.

157
00:13:29,373 --> 00:13:32,497
Read them when you do the lab yourself.

158
00:13:32,897 --> 00:13:37,009
Start with jersey and raise the ratio slowly from 1.

159
00:13:37,029 --> 00:13:40,426
The orange differential dog's stroke grows,

160
00:13:40,446 --> 00:13:44,875
the marks on the fabric between the dogs crowd together,

161
00:13:44,895 --> 00:13:50,358
and the sewn edge below goes from wavy to flat, then to fine gathers.

162
00:13:50,378 --> 00:13:56,237
Task one is to find the ratio that puts the edge strain in the green band.

163
00:13:56,637 --> 00:14:00,850
Task two, switch to rib. Rib stretches more,

164
00:14:00,870 --> 00:14:06,911
and less of the extra travel reaches the fabric. Find it again.

165
00:14:07,311 --> 00:14:11,860
Task three, chiffon, and it goes the other way.

166
00:14:11,880 --> 00:14:17,691
At a ratio of 1 the edge already puckers, so you go below 1.

167
00:14:18,091 --> 00:14:22,910
Finally, overdo it on purpose: turn jersey down for a lettuce edge.

168
00:14:22,930 --> 00:14:26,304
Turning chiffon up for gathers is yours to try.

169
00:14:26,324 --> 00:14:31,071
Record the readings at every step and compare them with the model.

170
00:14:32,591 --> 00:14:38,204
Virtual lab 9-2 is the spatial four-bar, also on the platform. The swing,

171
00:14:38,224 --> 00:14:44,224
the transmission angle and the other statistics, and the step-by-step results,

172
00:14:44,244 --> 00:14:45,844
are blurred here too.

173
00:14:46,244 --> 00:14:49,676
Pick the lower-looper preset. Task one:

174
00:14:49,696 --> 00:14:54,898
change only the crank radius until the swing is 30 degrees,

175
00:14:54,918 --> 00:15:00,739
then compare it with the rough estimate: swing is about 2r over c,

176
00:15:00,759 --> 00:15:05,784
where c is the rocker length. How far off is it, and why?

177
00:15:06,184 --> 00:15:11,448
Further down is the 3D view: grey crank, orange ball-joint link, blue rocker.

178
00:15:11,468 --> 00:15:14,399
The two axes neither meet nor are parallel;

179
00:15:14,419 --> 00:15:17,007
drag the view round to see it clearly.

180
00:15:17,027 --> 00:15:21,399
The later tasks move the rocker to raise the transmission angle,

181
00:15:21,419 --> 00:15:26,065
then increase r step by step until the mechanism can't be assembled.

